WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.783
In bobsleigh, luge and skeleton, collectively
known as the sliding sports

00:00:04.783 --> 00:00:06.599
crashes are an inevitability.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.480
"... Tearing down the hill!"

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:10.876
"Oh my goodness!"

00:00:10.876 --> 00:00:13.090
It's traumatic.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.530
It's aggressive.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:18.680
It's anything that is extreme times ten.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:26.070
To think that somebody has gone to the Olympics
without having had an episode like a crash

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.665
is very unlikely.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.844
Christina Smith is a trailblazer 
in the sport of bobsled.

00:00:34.844 --> 00:00:36.786
"...one of the most experienced drivers in
the field..."

00:00:36.786 --> 00:00:41.718
But in the years after she retired, she started
to experience troubling symptoms.

00:00:41.718 --> 00:00:46.376
Long-term, short-term memory loss, anxiety,
fear, depression.

00:00:46.376 --> 00:00:53.111
The inability to really stay focused 
and have emotional regulation.

00:00:53.111 --> 00:00:56.692
Eventually, she was diagnosed with 
a traumatic brain injury

00:00:56.692 --> 00:01:00.000
even though she’d never once 
been diagnosed with a concussion.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.212
Just the fact that I was losing something
of myself that I didn't realize

00:01:05.212 --> 00:01:10.000
potentially could have been contributed 
to the affects of 

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.310
the multiple runs, multiple vibrations,

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:21.225
the concussions that actually I'd never been
diagnosed with my entire career.

00:01:21.225 --> 00:01:24.513
There's growing evidence that, when it comes
to the sliding sports

00:01:24.513 --> 00:01:27.890
catastrophic concussions like 
the ones you get from crashing

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.821
might not be the main driver of brain injury.

00:01:30.821 --> 00:01:33.196
Microconcussions are.

00:01:33.196 --> 00:01:36.417
Concussions so mild they can often go unnoticed

00:01:36.417 --> 00:01:40.307
and so common that they have 
a special name: sled head.

00:01:42.258 --> 00:01:47.204
To understand how sled head happens, you have
to know how concussions work.

00:01:47.204 --> 00:01:50.901
Your brain is soft with a jello-like consistency.

00:01:50.901 --> 00:01:54.848
Normally, it’s pretty well protected 
by layers of bone and tissue.

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:59.334
But if your head gets hit or shook around
or just moves too fast,

00:01:59.334 --> 00:02:02.749
your brain can shift inside 
its protective casing

00:02:02.749 --> 00:02:05.113
bumping up against the sides of your skull.

00:02:05.113 --> 00:02:10.327
When that happens, the fragile neurons that
make up your brain can stretch or even tear.

00:02:10.327 --> 00:02:14.790
And when they tear, they release toxins that
can harm other neurons

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:20.292
triggering a chain reaction of damage, and
leaving your brain vulnerable to further injury.

00:02:20.292 --> 00:02:24.158
Concussions are serious and come with 
scary side effects.

00:02:24.158 --> 00:02:27.430
But for the most part they heal 
with time and rest.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:30.366
The problem is not all concussions are obvious.

00:02:30.366 --> 00:02:33.920
Hey, if you got out of that sled and you're
just a little bit dizzy

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.403
and then all of a sudden it's like,

00:02:35.403 --> 00:02:36.370
"No, I'm good."

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:37.584
And you're full of adrenaline.

00:02:37.584 --> 00:02:40.710
And essentially if you have the will and the
desire

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:45.000
to get back up and dust yourself off 
and do it again, so be it.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.365
But even a mild concussion is a concussion.

00:02:48.365 --> 00:02:52.940
And if it’s not given time to heal, the
damage can become more serious

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.100
and cause more enduring symptoms.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:03:00.960
That kind of scattered brain and, you know,
inability to focus.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.446
So a lot of those little things...

00:03:03.446 --> 00:03:06.572
It’s all those little things that 
make up sled head.

00:03:10.209 --> 00:03:13.228
From the outside, sledding sports look smooth.

00:03:13.228 --> 00:03:16.808
A graceful, if fast, run down an icy track.

00:03:16.808 --> 00:03:21.277
But, inside a sled, things look and feel 
very different.

00:03:21.277 --> 00:03:25.000
Sledding is, in a way, inherently brain rattling.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.521
Even during the smoothest ride, 
a sledder is tossed about. 

00:03:28.521 --> 00:03:31.143
Shaken, bumped, whipped around corners.

00:03:31.143 --> 00:03:35.770
It's so extreme that if you haven't 
gone to the bathroom

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:39.435
if you haven't blown your nose and if you
haven't stretched

00:03:39.435 --> 00:03:41.850
you have no idea what's going to 
happen to you.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:45.156
There's a lots of bouncing and 
people moving quickly.

00:03:45.156 --> 00:03:46.864
Their heads are going everywhere.

00:03:46.864 --> 00:03:49.530
Peter McCarthy is a neurophysiologist

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:54.240
whose research focuses on how various forces
effect our bodies.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.598
He told me that sledding is a lot like a very,
very intense roller coaster.

00:03:58.598 --> 00:04:03.344
They're rickety and they've got lots of vibrations
and they've got high g-turns.

00:04:03.344 --> 00:04:05.168
G, as in g-force.

00:04:05.168 --> 00:04:09.142
Roughly, the force of gravity 
or acceleration on an object.

00:04:09.142 --> 00:04:13.082
Going for a stroll on earth, you’re experiencing
1 g-force.

00:04:13.082 --> 00:04:16.290
Riding an average roller coaster, around 5.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:19.525
Military fighter pilots average 9 gs.

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:25.130
And the average football player receives 103
gs when hit during a game.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:29.192
The g-force of an average concussion 
is hard to measure or generalize

00:04:29.192 --> 00:04:35.931
but one researcher found that most diagnosed
ones have a g-force between 85 and 95.

00:04:35.931 --> 00:04:38.540
And, when another researcher 
measured the g-force

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:41.830
of his own skeleton ride at the 
Whistler Olympic track

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:47.037
at its highest, here, he recorded 84.5 g-force.

00:04:47.037 --> 00:04:50.271
That spike only happened for a few milliseconds.

00:04:50.271 --> 00:04:53.321
But spikes like it can happen with every run.

00:04:53.321 --> 00:04:56.545
And then there are the vibrations.

00:04:56.545 --> 00:05:01.190
Long term exposure to vibrations can lead
to a range of serious health issues

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:04.284
affecting basically all of your body’s systems.

00:05:04.284 --> 00:05:06.250
There are even international guidelines

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:10.342
about how much vibration someone should encounter
in a given period.

00:05:10.342 --> 00:05:14.060
And when Peter attached devices 
on the helmets, sleds, and backs

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:17.384
of skeleton athletes to measure how much they
were encountering

00:05:17.384 --> 00:05:20.690
preliminary results showed that 
doing just one run

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:23.256
could potentially put you over the limit.

00:05:23.256 --> 00:05:27.516
Sled Head is a phenomenon which seems to be
neurological in nature.

00:05:27.516 --> 00:05:32.290
And as a consequence ,we're thinking maybe
vibrations are getting through into the brain

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:34.880
and that could be the cause of it.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:39.230
All racing sports deal with both g-force and
vibration to a certain extent

00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:41.778
but normally they get dispersed 
throughout your body.

00:05:41.778 --> 00:05:46.141
When you’re in a sled there's not much between
your brain and the vibrations.

00:05:46.141 --> 00:05:49.760
I was so close and low that I had

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:54.200
chips of paint and scratches on the side of
my helmet from

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:57.534
just tapping going all the way down the track.

00:05:57.534 --> 00:06:03.380
And so, you know, of course, I get out and
I haven't had a crash or nothing would happen

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:07.882
but that just built up over time like a snowball.

00:06:07.882 --> 00:06:11.281
Years after she stopped sledding, 
Christina went to a specialist

00:06:11.281 --> 00:06:16.690
who ran a test called a quantitative electroencephalogram,
or qEEG.

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:22.110
The beauty of this is that it can actually
see where your brain is firing,

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:27.221
not firing at all, or partially or too much.

00:06:27.221 --> 00:06:30.030
When doctors analyzed her results, 
they saw this:

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:33.800
roughly, the red shading correlates 
to over-activation.

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:38.619
In a brain that hadn’t experienced trauma
those areas would be white.

00:06:38.619 --> 00:06:42.740
It was like a a-ha moment for me.

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:49.127
It really highlighted, you know what I had
been going through and I was no longer

00:06:49.127 --> 00:06:51.290
 hard on myself.

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:57.030
It was very common to overlook our own experiences
because we're always about

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:58.715
the show must go on.

00:06:58.715 --> 00:07:01.980
There’s only so much we can learn from 
one person’s brain scans.

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:03.669
Or the data from a few runs.

00:07:03.669 --> 00:07:08.330
It needs an objective assessment 
of the discipline, basically.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:12.090
Because if we just allow people 
to keep doing this

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:17.695
we don't know if it's safe for them to do
that with the intensity they're doing it.

00:07:17.695 --> 00:07:21.830
One way to better look after sliding athletes
is to require them to wear a monitor.

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:24.855
A simple sensor system could
detect an impact

00:07:24.855 --> 00:07:28.281
and have that per person immediately 
flagged for treatment

00:07:28.281 --> 00:07:31.895
and possibly sitting out until 
they've recovered fully.

00:07:31.895 --> 00:07:34.554
And there are some exciting developments 
in tech

00:07:34.554 --> 00:07:38.007
like virtual reality training 
that reduces ice time.

00:07:38.007 --> 00:07:42.010
Or an FDA-approved collar that increases 
blood volume in your brain

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:44.126
giving it better cushioning.

00:07:44.126 --> 00:07:48.459
In the future, new materials that could 
dampen vibration in the sled.

00:07:48.459 --> 00:07:53.420
But the federation that controls sliding sports
needs to make these available.

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:55.000
Or even mandatory.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:59.378
And I feel that that's something that hopefully
a way forward, though, who knows?

00:07:59.378 --> 00:08:02.977
It depends on international bodies to make
these legislative changes

00:08:02.977 --> 00:08:09.139
and start looking at their athletes as actually
things that keep the body alive.

00:08:09.139 --> 00:08:12.168
The athletes are the lifeblood of any sport

00:08:12.168 --> 00:08:15.000
and you need to protect them, in my mind.

